      "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+6}{2x+3}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14189988/assets/24f07da42a537ded7916fc2440dcf3ee1ccdfc81c7b027b47e97814fec059f5a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+6}{2x+3} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)+6}{2(0)+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
